<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lithuanian Jews</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Litvaks</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lithuanian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, or Litvaks, were the Jewish community of the lands of the historic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a>, known as the '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>'. From the 18th century they were known for Talmudic scholarship, led by figures such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilna-gaon">Vilna Gaon</a>, and for yeshivas such as Volozhin. About 90 to 95 per cent of the Jews of Lithuania were murdered in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a> in 1941–1944.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lithuanian-jews.jpg" alt="Lithuanian Jews" width="320" height="304" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mathiasrex, Maciej Szczepańczyk based on layers of Thomas Gun · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_showing_percentage_of_Jews_in_the_Pale_of_Settlement_in_the_Russian_Empire,_c._1905.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> from present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> are documented in the Polish-Lithuanian lands from the 14th century. In 1388 Vytautas granted them a charter that made them a class of freemen, answering in criminal cases directly to the grand duke and in petty suits standing on an equal footing with the lesser nobles. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-jagiellon">Alexander Jagiellon</a> expelled them in 1495 but let them return in 1503, and the Lithuanian Statute of 1566 imposed restrictions and distinctive dress, yellow caps for men and yellow kerchiefs for women.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmelnytsky-uprising">Khmelnytsky Uprising</a> destroyed the existing Jewish institutions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, yet the community grew from an estimated 120,000 in 1569 to about 250,000 in 1792. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-partition-of-poland">Second Partition</a> of 1793 the Litvaks became subjects of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. The word Litvak, from a Polish term for a man from Lithuania, was revived around 1880 in the narrower sense of a Lithuanian Jew.</p><p>Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> about 160,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, some 7 per cent of the population, with over 110 synagogues and 10 yeshivas in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> alone. Refugees from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> raised the number to about 250,000 by 1941. After the German invasion of June 1941, 141,000 Jews were murdered by the Nazis and Lithuanian collaborators, at sites such as the Paneriai woods and the Ninth Fort. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2005 counted 4,007 Jews in Lithuania.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Lithuanian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> was marked by intellectual <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Talmud</a> study, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> became the heartland of the misnagdim, the traditionalist opponents of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasidism">Hasidism</a>, though a sizeable minority of Litvaks were Hasidim of groups such as Chabad, Slonim and Karlin-Stolin. Many later followed the Haskala, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haskalah">Jewish Enlightenment</a>. Volozhin yeshiva became the model for most later yeshivas, and in Talmud study Chaim Soloveitchik and the Brisker school led. Litvaks have their own pronunciation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yiddish">Yiddish</a>, and in popular lore were set against the Galitzianers, down to their plainer, more savoury cooking. In Israel today, Lita'im is often used for any non-Hasidic Ashkenazi Haredi Jew.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Litvaks” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
